From 8dfefcbd034e7c72d302e411ee249d800757e4cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler South <tsouth2@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:01:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Give each display its own synced mode

Display sync applied every display's EDID mode through Omarchy's catch-all monitor rule, one after the other, so with two displays both outputs ended at the last display's mode. The first output no longer matched its window, and QEMU recreated the window of each output whose mode changed. The launcher put the recreated window back at its own size, which changed its EDID and started the next round, so the outputs flipped between the two sizes until the start timed out on a black wallpaper.

With several displays each output now gets its own rule. Hyprland cannot list its monitor rules, so display sync runs the user's Hyprland config under Lua with hl.monitor recording, from hyprland.lua through the modules it requires, and leaves alone every output a rule there names by connector or desc:. When the config does something it cannot follow, it leaves every output alone. A new rule for one output starts from Hyprland's defaults, so it carries the catch-all's other settings, such as transform, vrr and bitdepth. The display panel of newer Omarchy saves its choices by monitor in ~/.local/state/omarchy/monitors/settings and applies them as rules for the connector; a mode or scale chosen there stays. An output that keeps a rule of its own after the other displays go away follows its window too.

One display keeps the catch-all rule and the recorded mode for reloads. Revision 76 delivers the script to existing disks.
